WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.262
So far, everything on a page
has lived on one layer,

2
00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:05.519
stacked in the order we drew it.

3
00:00:05.617 --> 00:00:08.837
This time: layers, and a
few finishing touches.

4
00:00:08.922 --> 00:00:14.356
A border on one side, a title that is too
long, some artwork, and a greyscale proof.

5
00:00:14.564 --> 00:00:19.540
Here's the Recipe template, and the Layers
panel, just above Element Properties.

6
00:00:19.641 --> 00:00:23.996
Every template starts with one layer,
and everything we've drawn is on it,

7
00:00:23.996 --> 00:00:25.481
listed front to back.

8
00:00:25.687 --> 00:00:27.047
I'll make it two.

9
00:00:27.104 --> 00:00:30.114
Double-click the name to
rename it: Background.

10
00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.917
The plus adds a new layer on
top, which I'll call Content.

11
00:00:34.123 --> 00:00:37.703
The card stays on Background,
and everything else moves up.

12
00:00:37.843 --> 00:00:41.568
Click the first row, Shift-click
the last one above the card,

13
00:00:41.568 --> 00:00:43.405
and then Move to, Content.

14
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.190
Now lock Background.

15
00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:47.078
A locked layer still prints.

16
00:00:47.154 --> 00:00:51.672
It just ignores the mouse, so clicking
the page no longer grabs the card,

17
00:00:51.672 --> 00:00:55.574
and I can work on top of it
without nudging it by accident.

18
00:00:55.779 --> 00:00:58.149
Layers are good for
notes to yourself, too.

19
00:00:58.285 --> 00:01:01.285
A third layer, Notes,
with a reminder on it.

20
00:01:01.386 --> 00:01:04.611
The newest layer is also the
active one, shaded blue,

21
00:01:04.611 --> 00:01:06.991
so that's where the text box lands.

22
00:01:07.198 --> 00:01:09.028
Click the eye to hide the layer.

23
00:01:09.099 --> 00:01:12.319
It's gone from the canvas,
and from the PDF too.

24
00:01:12.452 --> 00:01:15.722
Hide your notes before you
export, and they never ship.

25
00:01:15.932 --> 00:01:18.542
I'd like a thin rule
under the Serves line.

26
00:01:18.626 --> 00:01:23.398
Per-Side Borders, in Appearance, draws
a border on just the sides you choose.

27
00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:28.208
Customize, then switch off top, right
and left, and make the bottom orange.

28
00:01:29.816 --> 00:01:31.086
One trap here.

29
00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:36.425
The PDF only draws per-side borders when
the box's own Stroke has a color and a

30
00:01:36.425 --> 00:01:39.449
width, and a text box starts with neither.

31
00:01:39.592 --> 00:01:44.772
The canvas shows the border either way,
so give Stroke a color, and a width of 1.

32
00:01:44.978 --> 00:01:46.618
Long titles are next.

33
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.214
In the hierarchy, I'll give
the Pad Thai a longer name:

34
00:01:50.214 --> 00:01:52.790
Weeknight Pad Thai with Crispy Tofu.

35
00:01:52.937 --> 00:01:55.877
On the page, the end of
it is simply cut off.

36
00:01:56.083 --> 00:02:00.263
Back on the template, I'll preview
the Pad Thai and select its title.

37
00:02:00.343 --> 00:02:05.303
Overflow, under Typography, decides
what happens when text doesn't fit.

38
00:02:05.361 --> 00:02:06.861
Clip is the default.

39
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.130
Ellipsis ends it with three dots.

40
00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:14.109
Shrink makes the text smaller until it
fits, which is usually what a title wants.

41
00:02:14.322 --> 00:02:18.734
The long name spills out of the grids
too: the strip along the bottom here,

42
00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:20.572
and the index on the cover.

43
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:23.873
Grids have their own
setting, Cell text overflow.

44
00:02:23.944 --> 00:02:25.324
Shrink, for both.

45
00:02:25.427 --> 00:02:29.432
Only the cells that need it get
smaller; the rest keep their size.

46
00:02:33.868 --> 00:02:36.048
Artwork can come in as SVG.

47
00:02:36.157 --> 00:02:39.582
New things land on the active
layer, the one shaded blue,

48
00:02:39.582 --> 00:02:42.297
and that is still Notes, which is hidden.

49
00:02:42.411 --> 00:02:44.341
So first I click Content.

50
00:02:44.395 --> 00:02:49.635
Then, from the SVG tools menu,
Import SVG file, and pick a whisk.

51
00:02:51.859 --> 00:02:54.987
It's a real vector, so it
stays sharp at any size,

52
00:02:54.987 --> 00:02:59.429
and it keeps its own colors, so
this one arrives in the same brown.

53
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.950
I'll make it 40 points square,
and tuck it under the heading.

54
00:03:06.306 --> 00:03:09.036
Most e-ink tablets show
only shades of grey.

55
00:03:09.087 --> 00:03:14.084
The contrast button beside Export PDF
switches the export to greyscale,

56
00:03:14.084 --> 00:03:20.175
so you can check that pale colors still
show up before the file goes onto a device.

57
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.149
The peach becomes a light grey,
the orange rules turn grey too,

58
00:03:24.149 --> 00:03:27.330
and the shaded row in the
index still stands out.

59
00:03:27.384 --> 00:03:30.957
On the last page, the long
title fits, the whisk is crisp,

60
00:03:30.957 --> 00:03:33.794
and the note we hid is nowhere to be seen.

61
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.420
Next time: real calendars, where every
month starts on the right weekday.
